## The problem to understand

The program manages a derived object through `std::unique_ptr<Base>`. Explain the lifetime defect, why using a smart pointer does not fix it, and two valid base-class designs depending on whether deletion through the base should be supported.

```cpp
struct Base {
    virtual void run() = 0;
    ~Base() = default;
};
struct Derived : Base {
    std::vector<int> data;
    void run() override {}
};
std::unique_ptr<Base> p = std::make_unique<Derived>();
```

## The mental model

The default deleter invokes `delete` on a `Base*`. In this ordinary hierarchy, deleting a derived object through a base pointer without a virtual destructor has undefined behavior. Ownership management and correct polymorphic destruction are separate requirements.

If polymorphic deletion is part of the API, give Base a public virtual destructor, usually `virtual ~Base() = default;`. If deletion through Base should be forbidden, a protected non-virtual destructor can enforce that contract, with ownership retained through a suitable concrete type or deliberate custom deleter. A virtual method elsewhere in the class does not make the destructor virtual.

## Check your understanding

- Identify deletion through Base* as the problematic operation.
- Explain the limitation of unique_ptr’s default deleter.
- Offer public virtual or protected non-virtual destructor designs with appropriate ownership.

## Think one step further

Why can shared_ptr<Base> constructed directly from make_shared<Derived>() behave differently?

## Primary reading

- [cppreference · Destructors](https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/destructor.html) · Check the virtual destructor requirement for deletion through a base pointer.
